# DTF tier manifest: f7qf-peer-authz  (GHSA-f7qf / 1.6.1)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single (the walking-skeleton topology). The peer
# data-plane authorization gap this tier proves is storage-independent — the
# missing check sits in the /api/v1/peers/* handlers, ahead of any storage I/O.
#
# The bug (GHSA-f7qf): the peer/federation data-plane MUTATING routes were
# nested under `auth_middleware` (authentication) but carried NO authorization
# guard. Any AUTHENTICATED non-admin could drive federation transfer/chunk/
# connection state: init a transfer session, mark a peer unreachable, or write
# chunk-availability rows. The fix adds `auth.require_admin()?` to the 9
# mutating handlers so a non-admin is refused with 403 BEFORE the handler body
# runs. The paired read GETs (e.g. GET /:id/connections) stay non-admin
# readable — the fix must not over-restrict them.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   BOUNDARY  a non-admin token on POST /:id/transfer/init,
#             POST /:id/connections/:target/unreachable, and
#             PUT  /:id/chunks/:artifact_id -> 403 (GREEN). On the vulnerable
#             baseline the request reaches the handler (2xx/404/500, NOT 403) =
#             the bypass. 403 (authz denied) is distinguished from 404/500
#             (handler reached) — that distinction is the whole tier.
#   CONTROL   admin on the same route -> passes authz (200/404/500, not 403);
#             a non-admin read GET /:id/connections -> 200 (not over-restricted);
#             anon on a mutating route -> 401 (auth_middleware still guards).
#   NOTE      ids are freshly-minted UUIDs (no peer need exist): the authz check
#             fires ahead of the handler body, so existence is irrelevant to the
#             boundary. `update_network_profile` is deliberately avoided — it is
#             a dead route (500s at path extraction), unrelated to this authz gap.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives an admin login + a user create +
# user login + a token mint + several back-to-back peer probes through one
# admin/user pair; the base default (ON) would throttle them. A tier that must
# disable a shipped control does so explicitly (design 2.2, as isolation/sso do).
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
